---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import "../styles/global.css";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Restaurant",
  name: "Sierra Mar",
  servesCuisine: ["Californian"],
  priceRange: "$$$$",
  telephone: "+1-831-667-2800",
  url: "https://www.postranchinn.com/dining",
  address: {
    "@type": "PostalAddress",
    streetAddress: "47900 State Hwy 1",
    addressLocality: "Big Sur",
    addressRegion: "CA",
    postalCode: "93920",
    addressCountry: "US",
  },
  description:
    "Cliffside fine-dining restaurant inside Post Ranch Inn in Big Sur, led by chef Il Hoon Kang, serving seasonal prix-fixe Californian tasting menus with a celebrated wine program.",
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sierra Mar Menu Guide (2026) — Prices, What to Order | Hotel Menu Guide"
  description="An independent guide to Sierra Mar at Post Ranch Inn in Big Sur: the prix-fixe tasting menu with reported prices, the wine program, what we'd order, and visiting tips. Prices reported as of October 2026."
  path="/sierra-mar-menu/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogImage="/images/sierra-mar-dining.webp"
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6">
    <img
      src="/images/sierra-mar-dining.webp"
      alt="Illustration of Sierra Mar's cliffside dining room overlooking the Pacific at Big Sur"
      class="w-full h-64 sm:h-96 object-cover rounded-2xl mt-8"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />

    <p class="mt-8 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-gold-dark">
      Menu Guide · Big Sur
    </p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold mt-2 leading-tight">
      Sierra Mar — Menu Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-ink-soft">By <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Firoz Khan</span> <span class="text-gold-dark">&middot;</span> FK Digital Media</p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      Perched on the cliffs above the Pacific along Highway 1, Sierra Mar is the dining room of
      Post Ranch Inn — a destination restaurant where the meal is the point of the trip, not an
      add-on to it. Executive chef Il Hoon Kang serves one prix-fixe tasting menu at dinner and
      a family-style feast at lunch, both built around organic, seasonal Californian cooking.
      There is no à la carte menu, so this guide covers what the experience costs, how it works,
      and what the food is like — all written in our own words, with prices reported from official
      sources rather than copied from a menu.
    </p>

    <!-- At a glance -->
    <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl border border-line bg-white p-6 sm:p-8">
      <h2 class="!mt-0 font-display text-xl font-bold">At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-sm">
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Location</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">47900 State Hwy 1, Big Sur, CA 93920 (inside Post Ranch Inn)</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Chef</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Il Hoon Kang</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Cuisine</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Organic Californian, seasonal farm-to-table</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Price level</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">$$$$ — prix fixe only: $85 lunch / $185 dinner, per person</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Hours</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">Lunch daily 12–3 PM; dinner daily 5:30–9 PM</dd></div>
        <div><dt class="font-semibold text-ink">Phone</dt><dd class="text-ink-soft">+1 (831) 667-2800</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-guide">
      <h2>How the meal works</h2>
      <p>
        Sierra Mar does not publish an à la carte menu — there are no individual dish prices to
        list, because you don't order off a menu here. Lunch is a <strong>family-style experience
        reported at $85 per person</strong>, served daily from 12 to 3 PM. Dinner is a
        <strong>seasonal tasting menu reported at $185 per person</strong>, served daily from
        5:30 to 9 PM. A supplemental sustainable caviar service is offered for an additional
        <strong>reported $220</strong>. The lineup shifts with the seasons, so even the structure
        of the tasting changes over the year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Advance reservations are required and are bookable on OpenTable; the room is in high
        demand, so don't count on walking in. Note for non-hotel guests: the inn's gatehouse
        admits dining guests at most 30 minutes before their reservation, so plan your arrival
        along Highway 1 accordingly.
      </p>

      <h2>What the food is like</h2>
      <p>
        The cooking is organic, Californian, and relentlessly seasonal — the menu is built
        around what's ripe in Big Sur and Monterey County right now rather than around signature
        dishes that stay put year to year. The kitchen describes its approach as a dialogue of
        balance: between land and sea, richness and restraint, with sustainability as a constant
        rather than a theme. Expect the meal to move from the garden to the coast and back —
        vegetables treated with the same seriousness as the fish.
      </p>
      <p>
        The supply chain is part of the story: the restaurant works with local partners including
        Ad Astra Bread Co., The Cheese Shop in Carmel, Brokaw Farms in Soledad, and Monterey
        Abalone — growers and producers from the same coastline the dining room overlooks. The
        whole point is that the meal could only come from here.
      </p>

      <h2>The wine program</h2>
      <p>
        Sierra Mar's cellar is one of the most decorated on the California coast. It holds the
        <strong>Wine Spectator Grand Award</strong> — the only one in Monterey County — with a
        reported <strong>3,200+ selections</strong> drawn from a <strong>12,000-bottle cellar</strong>.
        If you're serious about wine, this is as much a draw as the food: the list runs deep
        into coastal California and well beyond it. Ask the sommelier to pair with the tasting —
        that's the room's natural strength.
      </p>

      <h2>Recognition — one honest note</h2>
      <p>
        Sierra Mar holds a <strong>Michelin Green Star</strong>, and we want to be precise about
        what that is: the Green Star is Michelin's award for <strong>environmental and
        sustainability practices</strong>, not a culinary star. Press coverage in July 2026 was
        explicit that the restaurant has not received a Michelin culinary star — the Green Star
        honors how the kitchen sources, farms, and operates. Separately, it was named
        <strong>Best Restaurant in Big Sur 2025</strong> in the Monterey County Weekly readers'
        poll.
      </p>

      <h2>What we'd order</h2>
      <p>
        There's only one decision tree here, so think of it as how we'd play the visit rather
        than what we'd pick off a menu:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Do dinner, not lunch, if you can only go once.</strong> The evening tasting is the full expression of the kitchen — the family-style lunch is excellent, but dinner is the reason the room has its reputation.</li>
        <li><strong>Add the caviar service.</strong> At a reported $220 supplement on a $185 tasting, it's a serious splurge — but the kitchen's sustainability ethos makes its caviar sourcing genuinely interesting, not just luxurious.</li>
        <li><strong>Let the sommelier drive.</strong> A 3,200-selection Grand Award list is not meant to be navigated alone. Hand the pairing over and tell them what you like.</li>
        <li><strong>Book for the light.</strong> A 5:30 PM seating in summer puts you over the Pacific at golden hour — the view is part of the menu.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Planning your visit</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Book well ahead.</strong> Dinner reservations are required and the room fills — OpenTable shows it booking dozens of times a day.</li>
        <li><strong>Time the drive.</strong> Non-hotel guests are admitted at the gatehouse a maximum of 30 minutes before the reservation; Highway 1 is slow and beautiful, so give yourself more time than the map says.</li>
        <li><strong>Budget honestly:</strong> $185 per person for the tasting plus the wine program means a comfortable dinner for two lands well above $500 before tip — with the caviar supplement, well above $900.</li>
        <li><strong>Dress code</strong> is smart casual, and valet parking is available.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Price disclaimer -->
    <div class="mt-10 rounded-xl border border-gold/40 bg-gold/10 p-5 text-sm text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p class="font-semibold text-ink mb-1">About these prices</p>
      <p>
        Prices above are <strong>reported as of October 2026</strong>, compiled from the restaurant's
        OpenTable listing and July 2026 press coverage. Sierra Mar is prix-fixe only, so there are
        no à la carte dish prices to report. Menus change seasonally and prices move — always
        verify current prices with the restaurant before visiting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-8 text-sm text-ink-soft">
      <a href="/" class="text-gold-dark font-semibold hover:underline">← Back to all guides</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
